\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

A digital stethoscope worn by a patient could listen continuously and flag wheezes, crackles or stridor the moment they occur, instead of waiting for the next spot check. Such a monitor must run in a streaming fashion: every few tens of milliseconds a new chunk of audio arrives and must be processed within a fixed latency and energy budget, without missing short or rare abnormal sounds. Today's respiratory sound models are accurate~\cite{bardou2018lung,petmezas2022automated,niizumi2025towards}, but they are designed for offline analysis of whole recordings, and they spend the same amount of computation on every frame. Most of that computation is wasted. Between transients, the spectrum in most frequency bands changes little from one 40\,ms step to the next, and only some bands carry information that matters for the current decision. A streaming detector should therefore observe all audio cheaply, but recompute its expensive internal representation only where doing so changes the result.

Existing methods do not solve this selection problem well. Conditional-computation methods for recurrent and attention models skip work when an input or activation changes little~\cite{neil2017delta}, or learn skip gates end to end with a budget penalty~\cite{campos2018skip,jernite2017variable,seo2018neural}. Change-based rules assume that large changes are the important ones, which is not true for auscultation: a handling artefact moves the spectrum a lot, while a faint crackle barely moves it. Temporal graph networks keep per-node memories~\cite{seo2018structured,rossi2020temporal}, but they update every node at every step or event and have no notion of a compute budget. AGN-Net~\cite{islam2026agnnet} came closest. It updates only a localized region of a temporal graph around high-priority nodes and carries the other node states forward. Its learned priority, however, is trained to predict how much a node's representation will change, and that study reported that this priority neither tracked the realized change nor beat random selection by much. In short, no existing method tells a streaming model \emph{which} expensive updates it can safely skip.

We propose \emph{Event-Aware AGN-Net}, a selection method named after the localized-update model it builds on, which learns this directly. Our key idea is to change what the priority predicts: instead of predicting where a representation will change, we predict where \emph{skipping} its update would harm the task. The model keeps a persistent graph of 64 frequency-region nodes and runs a cheap encoder on every 40\,ms chunk. Under a fixed budget, it updates the node states of only a small set of regions. A lightweight controller chooses those regions. It is trained on a counterfactual \emph{skip-harm utility}, the change in the model's own output when a region's update is withheld, so the controller learns which updates the prediction actually depends on.

Our contribution is a selection method, not a new backbone. First, we introduce the skip-harm utility as a training target for update controllers, together with a budget-exact, cost-aware greedy rule that counts the neighbours each selected region pulls in and the refreshes of stale states. The method makes no assumption about the recurrent cell or the graph; we instantiate it in a streaming acoustic graph model in which an always-on encoder observes all audio and an AGN-Net-style cell updates persistent frequency-region states only on a budget-limited, induced subgraph. Second, we give a conditional bound that relates selective-update error to skipped and boundary defects, measure its assumptions on real trajectories, and link it to the output-level objective. Third, we evaluate the method with two cells, two graph topologies and two datasets (the paediatric SPRSound benchmark and the adult HF\_Lung\_V1 database), with three seeds and patient-level statistics. Our primary measure is \emph{preservation}: how much of what the full-update model detects survives selective inference. Absolute accuracy against the annotations is a secondary check, because on SPRSound it is dominated by an annotation-style shift between training and test data. With a quarter of the graph updates, our controller preserves the full-update model's detections with an event-preservation F of {{agn_C_util@0.25_test2024_fidF|2f}}, against {{agn_C_e2e@0.25_test2024_fidF|2f}} for a gate trained end to end on the task loss, {{agn_C_change@0.25_test2024_fidF|2f}} for AGN-Net's change objective and {{agn_C_random@0.25_test2024_fidF|2f}} for random selection. The gain over the end-to-end gate holds in every seed for both cells, and the gain over the change objective and random selection also holds on the harmonic graph and on HF\_Lung. For the AGN-Net cell on SPRSound, the selective model's event F against the annotations is non-inferior to full updating at this budget; in the other settings it is missed at 25\% on the 2024 set or on HF\_Lung (narrowly for the graph GRU on SPRSound and the AGN-Net cell on HF\_Lung, more clearly for the harmonic graph and for the graph GRU on HF\_Lung) and holds at 50\% in all settings. Fourth, we show that the one-step target ranks regions well by their multi-step harm, that on long streams the refresh threshold should depend on the budget, that harmonic graph edges stabilise the AGN-Net cell, and how the controller's overhead shrinks as the cell grows. We also report what does not work: the AGN-Net cell itself is not a better detector than a plain graph GRU, and at our model size the end-to-end speedup is modest ({{rt_numpy_util@0.25_speedup|2f}}$\times$) and depends on the runtime.

The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work. Section~\ref{sec:method} presents the method, Section~\ref{sec:experiments} the experimental setup, and Section~\ref{sec:results} the results. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.
